    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Azerbaijani culture in Iran encompasses Azerbaijani Turkish, regional performance traditions, urban commercial life, and distinctive crafts. Its communities extend beyond northwestern Iran, while Iranian Azerbaijan itself contains multiple languages and identities.",
    "tags": ["Azerbaijani culture", "Iranian Azerbaijan", "Tabriz", "Ashiq", "Textile crafts"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:09:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:09:07Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Azerbaijani Culture in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Azerbaijani culture in Iran through ashiq storytelling, Tabriz’s bazaar, regional crafts, and overlapping identities."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "region-and-community",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A regional culture with connections beyond the region",
        "content": "Here, Azerbaijani culture refers to communities and traditions in Iran, rather than serving as another name for the culture of the Republic of Azerbaijan. Iranian Azerbaijan is a major Azerbaijani Turkish-speaking region, but it is not linguistically uniform. Richard Tapper’s account identifies Kurdish and Tati-speaking communities within it and describes Azerbaijani settlement elsewhere in Iran. [s1](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/azerbaijan-vi/)\n\nRegional belonging consequently has several dimensions. A person’s language, home city, family history, and Iranian identity need not compete with one another. Tapper’s historical analysis also shows that tribal affiliation and language could change. This makes culture more intelligible as a history of relationships than as an unchanging inheritance shared identically by everyone. [s1](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/azerbaijan-vi/)",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ashiq-performance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The ashiq: poetry, music, and storytelling",
        "content": "An ashiq is a poet-musician who accompanies singing on the saz, a long-necked plucked instrument. Charlotte Albright’s research describes this performance tradition in Iranian Azerbaijan and distinguishes practices associated with Tabriz and Urmia. In the settings she studied before the revolution, performers around Tabriz could work with accompanying musicians, whereas Urmia’s tradition emphasized solo performance. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/aseq/)\n\nThe repertoire includes short poems and dastans: extended heroic or romantic narratives combining speech and song. The performer can narrate an episode in a speaking voice and sing a character’s words, making the movement between storytelling and music central to the experience. Coffeehouses, weddings, and festive gatherings appear in Albright’s historical account as important performance settings. These descriptions document particular practices and periods; they do not establish a uniform performance format today. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/aseq/)",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tabriz-bazaar",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Tabriz: commerce as a cultural setting",
        "content": "Tabriz’s historic bazaar illustrates an urban dimension of regional culture. UNESCO describes interconnected covered brick structures and spaces accommodating different functions, and evaluates the complex as a commercial and cultural system. Its significance extends beyond the appearance of individual buildings to the relationships sustained within a functioning marketplace. [s3](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1346/)\n\nThis is a useful counterpoint to accounts centered exclusively on costumes or rural customs. A bazaar places cultural history within work, exchange, and the use of shared urban space. UNESCO’s heritage assessment supports the importance of this particular complex; it does not measure the cultural preferences or everyday routines of all residents of Tabriz. [s3](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1346/)",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "crafts-and-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Craft traditions change with their markets",
        "content": "Osku, a town in Iranian Azerbaijan, is associated in craft scholarship with batik-dyed headscarves. Batik uses a resist, such as wax or paste, to protect selected areas of fabric during dyeing. The historical account describes an expansion from established scarf designs into additional colors, patterns, and products for new buyers. [s4](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/crafts/)\n\nThat example captures a broader editorial point: innovation does not automatically sever a craft from its history. Understanding the maker, technique, market, and date is more informative than asking whether an object looks sufficiently old. Together, performance, commercial architecture, and textile work reveal distinct settings in which Azerbaijani culture has been made and remade within Iran. [s2](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/aseq/) [s3](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1346/) [s4](https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/crafts/)",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
